{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_dv3sfcyxjtpzknpbcorkvcxbvzqouikwqtdkt5drto65blaveluq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The translation is complete. Here's a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated the C SATC (Separating Axis Theorem Collision detection) library to safe, idiomatic Rust. All `unimplemented!()` placeholders in `src/satc.rs` have been replaced with working implementations.\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n\n1. **Memory management**: The C code uses raw pointers and `malloc`/`free`. The Rust version uses owned `Vec<[f64; 2]>` and `[f64; 2]` arrays — no `unsafe`, no FFI, no `libc`.\n\n2. **Mutating vs returning**: Most C \"point\" functions mutate their first argument and return it. Since the Rust signatures take `&[f64; 2]` (immutable refs) and return `[f64; 2]`, I implemented them as pure functions that compute and return new values.\n\n3. **Polygon recalc**: I added a private helper `_satc_polygon_recalc` (matching C's `_satc_polygon_recalc`) that recomputes `calc_points`, `edges`, and `normals`. It's called by `SatcPolygon::new` / `satc_polygon_set_points`.\n\n4. **Faithful reproduction**: I preserved a couple of quirks from the C reference (e.g., `satc_polygon_get_aabb`'s `y_max = x_max` initialization and `satc_point_rotate`'s `x*sin - y*cos` formula) to match the C behavior exactly.\n\n5. **Verification**: I ported the test cases from `c_src/satc-test.c` to a Rust integration test and confirmed all assertions pass (point scaling, centroids of square/triangle, circle-circle and polygon-circle collisions, point-in-circle, point-in-polygon including the small hexagon case). The test file was removed after verification.\n\nThe library compiles cleanly with no errors and only a few standard clippy style suggestions."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":146,"cache_read_input_tok